A race condition is when the result depends on the unpredictable timing of two or more processes touching the same resource, so the outcome is right on some runs and wrong on others. With a shared log, two scripts writing at once can interleave their output mid-line or, if either one rewrites the file rather than appending, lose entries.

Take an exclusive lock around the write with `flock`:

```sh
log() {
    {
        flock -x 9
        printf '%s %s\n' "$(date -Is)" "$*" >&9
    } 9>>/var/log/myapp.log
}
```

Every writer must go through the same lock for this to work; the lock is released when the block closes file descriptor 9, even if the script dies.

Always open with `>>`, never `>`: append mode makes the kernel move each write to the end of the file atomically, so a single short `printf` line from each writer will not overwrite another's. That alone is enough for one-line entries on a local filesystem; the lock is what you need for multi-line entries, or for read-then-write steps such as "rotate if larger than N".

`flock` is unreliable on NFS, so there have every host log to a local file or send lines to `logger` (syslog) instead.
